.\" Manual page for detcd.  See the comment at the top of detc.8 for why these
.\" are roff and not markdown put through a converter.
.TH DETCD 8 2026\-08\-04 "detc 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.
.SH NAME
detcd \- answer for a detc system over varlink
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B detcd
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-read\-only ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B detcd
is not a daemon.  It speaks varlink on the connection it was handed, answers
the call, and exits.  Nothing listens between two calls, so there is nothing to
enable, nothing resident, and nothing left behind on the machine that answered.
.PP
It is the same program as
.BR detc (8),
under a second name, and every method it answers is one of that program's
subcommands run locally.  There is nothing it can do that
.B detc
cannot, and nothing
.B detc
can do that it will not \(em except what
.B \-\-read\-only
takes away.
.PP
Two conventions for handing it a connection are read, and neither is
configured:
.B $LISTEN_FDS
in the manner of a socket activated service, which is how
.RB \(lq "varlinkctl exec:" \(rq
starts it, and otherwise the standard input and output, which is what a pair of
pipes over
.BR ssh (1)
gives it.  The variables that announce the socket are taken out of the
environment before anything else runs, so that a probe or a provider started by
the call does not inherit them and mistake itself for a service.
.PP
The interface is
.BR org.detc.Manager ,
and the service answers for it by introspection, so a client needs nothing
installed to read it:
.PP
.EX
$ varlinkctl introspect exec:/usr/bin/detcd org.detc.Manager
.EE
.PP
It is also a file,
.IR /usr/share/varlink/org.detc.Manager.varlink ,
for a code generator or a reader.  Nothing opens it at runtime \(em the binary
carries its own copy \(em so a machine that has only the binary is missing
nothing.
.
.SS Authorization
There is none, and that is the design.
.B detcd
runs as whoever started it, and the filesystem stops it exactly where it stops
.BR detc ,
so whoever can run
.B detcd
on a machine can already run
.B detc
on it.  What encrypts the conversation, proves who is at the other end and
keeps the keys is
.BR ssh (1),
which does all three better than this program could.
.PP
What
.B \-\-read\-only
adds belongs on the far side too, in the line of
.I authorized_keys
that admits the key, where the caller cannot talk it out of it:
.PP
.EX
command="/usr/bin/detcd \-\-read\-only",no\-pty ssh\-ed25519 AAAA... monitoring
.EE
.PP
Which tree is configured is decided by whoever starts the service and never by
the caller: there is no method that asks for another root.  The one thing that
travels is a bundle, and a bundle carries its own signature, checked against
the
.I allowed_signers
of the machine that installs it.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-root " " \fIPATH\fR
Answer for a system mounted somewhere else instead of
.IR / .
.TP
.B \-\-read\-only
Refuse every method that changes the system \(em
.BR Apply
without
.IR dry_run ,
.BR SetVariables ,
.BR MergeDocument ,
.B InstallBundle
and
.B RemoveBundle
\(em with the
.B ReadOnly
error.  See
.B Authorization
above for where it belongs.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Turn logging on, and repeat for more of it.  It goes to the standard error,
which is not the connection.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.
.SH METHODS
Every method is a subcommand of
.BR detc (8).
.B ListTypes
and
.B List
answer
.BR "detc list" ;
.B Cat
answers
.BR "detc cat" ;
.B Check
answers
.BR "detc check" ;
.B Doc
and
.B Schema
answer the commands of the same name;
.BR GetVariables ,
.BR SetVariables ,
.BR MergeDocument ,
.B ListProbes
and
.B RunProbe
answer
.BR "detc var" ;
.BR ListRuns ,
.B GetRun
and
.B GetFailures
answer
.BR "detc report" ;
.BR VerifyBundle ,
.BR GetBundle ,
.B InstallBundle
and
.B RemoveBundle
answer
.BR "detc bundle" ;
and
.B Apply
answers
.BR "detc apply" .
.PP
Most of them stream: they send one reply per line of what the subcommand
prints, so they want
.B more
and are refused without it with the
.B ExpectedMore
error.  A stream ends with a reply whose field is null, so that the end needs
no lookahead and a run with nothing to say is not a special case.
.PP
A call that failed comes back as the
.B Failed
error, carrying the message
.B detc
would have printed.  The full interface, with the types and every argument, is
what
.RB \(lq "varlinkctl introspect" \(rq
prints, and what the
.B .varlink
file named above holds.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The call was answered.
.TP
.B 1
The call was answered with an error reply, which is the status
.B detc
would have exited with, or the connection could not be spoken on.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/varlink/
The interfaces this service speaks:
.I org.detc.Manager.varlink
and the standard
.IR org.varlink.service.varlink ,
for a client that is being written against them.  Neither is read by anything
at runtime.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR LISTEN_FDS ", " LISTEN_PID
The socket to answer on, in the manner of
.BR sd_listen_fds (3).
Exactly one descriptor is a connection; anything else is a shape this program
has nothing to do with, and it falls back to the standard input and output.
Both are unset before the call runs.
.PP
The variables of
.BR detc (8)
apply as well, and are read at the same points.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask a host what it would change, without changing it:
.PP
.EX
$ varlinkctl call \-\-more ssh\-exec:web1:/usr/bin/detcd \e
      org.detc.Manager.Apply '{"dry_run":true}'
.EE
.PP
Read a variable of a host over a connection that cannot change it:
.PP
.EX
$ varlinkctl call ssh\-exec:web1:"/usr/bin/detcd \-\-read\-only" \e
      org.detc.Manager.GetVariables '{"key":["ssh"]}'
.EE
.PP
For a fleet rather than a host, see
.BR detctl (8),
which is a client of this and drives many at once.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR detc (8),
.BR detctl (8),
.BR varlinkctl (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR sshd (8),
.BR sd_listen_fds (3)
